    body: `
<p>English says "I want", "I need", "I can", with the doer first and the same "I" every time. Tagalog puts the wanting word first, and the person who wants takes the <b>ng form</b>: <b>ko</b>, <b>mo</b>, <b>niya</b>, or <b>ni</b> before a name. Think of <b>Gusto ko</b> as "my want is…".</p>
<h3>Wanting a thing: gusto + ng form + ng + thing</h3>
<p>The thing you want takes the <b>ng</b> marker, just like the thing after a verb.</p>
{{ex:Gusto ko ng kape.|goos-TOH koh nahng kah-PEH|I want coffee.}}
{{ex:Gusto ni Ana ng mangga.|goos-TOH nee AH-nah nahng mahng-GAH|Ana wants a mango.}}
<h3>Wanting to do something: add the linker</h3>
<p>To want to <i>do</i> something, put the verb after the pronoun and join them with the linker <b>-ng</b>: <b>ko</b> becomes <b>kong</b>, <b>mo</b> becomes <b>mong</b>, <b>niya</b> becomes <b>niyang</b>. Use the basic form of the verb. For -um- verbs it looks like the completed form (<b>kumain</b>, <b>uminom</b>, <b>umuwi</b>); for mag- verbs it is the mag- form (<b>magluto</b>, <b>magtrabaho</b>). If a little word such as <b>ba</b> comes before the verb, the linker moves onto it: <b>bang</b>.</p>
{{ex:Gusto kong kumain.|goos-TOH kohng koo-MAH-een|I want to eat.}}
{{ex:Gusto mo bang uminom?|goos-TOH moh bahng oo-mee-NOHM|Do you want to drink something?}}
<h3>ayaw and kailangan work the same way</h3>
<p><b>ayaw</b> is "not want", so it needs no <b>hindi</b>. <b>Ayaw ko</b> is usually said as one word, <b>ayoko</b>, and with the linker <b>ayokong</b>. <b>kailangan</b> is "need".</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th></th><th>a thing</th><th>an action</th></tr><tr><td>want</td><td class="k">Gusto ko ng tubig.</td><td class="k">Gusto kong umuwi.</td></tr><tr><td>don't want</td><td class="k">Ayaw ko ng tubig.</td><td class="k">Ayokong umuwi.</td></tr><tr><td>need</td><td class="k">Kailangan ko ng tubig.</td><td class="k">Kailangan kong umuwi.</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:Ayaw ko ng gatas.|AH-yow koh nahng GAH-tahs|I don't want milk.}}
{{ex:Kailangan mong umuwi.|kah-ee-LAH-ngahn mohng oo-moo-WEEQ|You need to go home.}}
<h3>puwede and marunong take the ang form</h3>
<p>Two "can" words are different. <b>puwede</b> (also written <b>pwede</b>) means "may, it's allowed, it's possible". <b>marunong</b> means "know how to". With the -um- and mag- verbs you know, both take the ang form: <b>ako</b>, <b>ka</b>, <b>siya</b>, joined to the verb with the linker. So "I can speak Tagalog" is about skill: <b>Marunong akong mag-Tagalog</b>. <i>Puwede akong mag-Tagalog</i> would mean "I'm allowed to speak Tagalog".</p>
{{ex:Puwede ba akong kumain?|PWEH-deh bah ah-KOHNG koo-MAH-een|May I eat?}}
{{ex:Marunong akong mag-Tagalog.|mah-ROO-nohng ah-KOHNG mahg-tah-GAH-lohg|I can speak Tagalog.}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> using the ang form after gusto, and leaving out the linker. <i>Gusto ako ng kape</i> → <b>Gusto ko ng kape</b>; <i>Gusto ko kumain</i> → <b>Gusto kong kumain</b>.</div>
`,
