    id: "time",
    title: "Time and days: alas-tres, Lunes, kahapon, bukas",
    blurb: "Tell the time, say how long something takes, and talk about days, yesterday, later and tomorrow.",
    deck: "Time, days and dates",
    body: `
<p>English uses one set of numbers for everything. Tagalog uses two: Spanish numbers for the clock, native Tagalog numbers for counting how long.</p>
<h3>The clock: alas- + Spanish number</h3>
<p>To ask the time, say <b>Anong oras na?</b> (literally "What hour already?"). The answer is <b>alas-</b> plus the Spanish number: <b>alas-dos</b>, <b>alas-tres</b>, up to <b>alas-dose</b>. One o'clock is the exception: <b>ala-una</b>, with no s. Half past is <b>y medya</b>.</p>
{{ex:Anong oras na?|ah-NOHNG OH-rahs nah|What time is it?}}
{{ex:Alas-tres na.|ah-lahs-TREHS nah|It's three o'clock.}}
<p>Add the part of the day with <b>ng</b>: <b>ng umaga</b> (in the morning), <b>ng hapon</b> (in the afternoon), <b>ng gabi</b> (in the evening).</p>
{{ex:Alas-siyete y medya ng umaga.|ah-lahs-SHEH-teh ee MEH-jah nahng oo-MAH-gah|Half past seven in the morning.}}
<h3>How long: native number + linker</h3>
<p>For a length of time, count with native numbers, linked to the noun as always: <b>tatlong oras</b> (three hours), <b>limang minuto</b> (five minutes), <b>dalawang araw</b> (two days). So <b>alas-tres</b> is a time on the clock, and <b>tatlong oras</b> is three hours long.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th></th><th>clock (Spanish)</th><th>how long (native)</th></tr><tr><td>3</td><td class="k">alas-tres</td><td class="k">tatlong oras</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td class="k">alas-singko</td><td class="k">limang oras</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td class="k">alas-otso</td><td class="k">walong oras</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:Limang minuto lang.|lee-MAHNG mee-NOO-toh lahng|Just five minutes.}}
<h3>Days of the week</h3>
<p>The days come from Spanish and are written with a capital: <b>Lunes, Martes, Miyerkoles, Huwebes, Biyernes, Sabado, Linggo</b>. "On Monday" is <b>sa Lunes</b>, with the sa marker, the same one you use for places.</p>
{{ex:Pupunta ako sa Makati sa Sabado.|poo-poon-TAH ah-KOH sah mah-KAH-tee sah SAH-bah-doh|I'm going to Makati on Saturday.}}
<h3>Yesterday, today, tomorrow</h3>
<p><b>kahapon</b> (yesterday), <b>kanina</b> (earlier today), <b>ngayon</b> (now, today), <b>mamaya</b> (later today), <b>bukas</b> (tomorrow). The verb's aspect has to agree: completed with <b>kahapon</b> and <b>kanina</b>, future with <b>mamaya</b> and <b>bukas</b>. Careful: <b>mamaya</b> looks like "maybe", but it means "later today".</p>
{{ex:Kumain ako kanina.|koo-MAH-een ah-KOH kah-NEE-nah|I ate earlier.}}
{{ex:Kakain tayo mamaya.|kah-KAH-een TAH-yoh mah-mah-YAHQ|We'll eat later.}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> using a Spanish number for a length of time, or a native one on the clock. <i>tres oras</i> → <b>tatlong oras</b> (three hours); <i>alas-tatlo</i> → <b>alas-tres</b> (three o'clock).</div>
`,
